{"id":"interconnect","corpus":"aws","title":"AWS Interconnect","sourceUrl":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/interconnect/latest/userguide/what-is-interconnect.html","license":"AWS Documentation © Amazon Web Services, Inc.","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"AWS Interconnect"},{"kind":"h2","text":"What is AWS Interconnect?"},{"kind":"p","text":"AWS Interconnect is a family of managed private connectivity services that enables you to create high-speed connections from your locations to AWS and between cloud environments."},{"kind":"p","text":"With AWS Interconnect, you no longer need to configure physical or virtual routers, order cross-connects, or manage BGP peering. Through a simplified process you select your AWS region, your required network capacity, and your preferred provider."},{"kind":"p","text":"AWS and the provider will quickly provision and configure your requested capacity on redundant network devices. They present it to you as a single object called an interconnect."},{"kind":"p","text":"AWS Interconnect includes two offerings:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Interconnect – multicloud: Connect your AWS VPCs directly to VPCs in other public clouds."},{"kind":"li","text":"Interconnect – last mile: Connect your branch offices, data centers, and remote locations to AWS through qualified delivery partners' existing last-mile networks."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Advantages of AWS Interconnect"},{"kind":"h3","text":"Simplified Network Architecture"},{"kind":"p","text":"With AWS Interconnect, your traffic is transported on the AWS global backbone until it is handed off to the provider directly. This eliminates the need for complex on-premises routing or intermediate network hops."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Fast Provisioning and Scaling"},{"kind":"p","text":"New Interconnects are typically provisioned and configured within minutes. Once live, you can increase or decrease bandwidth directly through the AWS Console without reprovisioning connections or engaging support. This elastic model lets you scale up for peak workloads and scale back down to control costs."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Fully managed service"},{"kind":"p","text":"AWS and your provider manage all aspects of the physical network infrastructure and configuration. This includes provisioning network devices, configuring VLANs and BGP sessions, and handling ongoing maintenance. You focus on your applications while AWS handles the underlying connectivity infrastructure."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Built-in maximum resiliency"},{"kind":"p","text":"Every Interconnect is provisioned across redundant network devices spanning at least two physically distinct facilities with independent power and networking. This architecture eliminates single points of failure at the device, cross-connect, and facility level. Multicloud and last mile connections use a four-connection model with Equal-Cost Multi-Path (ECMP) load balancing, ensuring at least one link remains operational during planned maintenance."},{"kind":"h2","text":"How AWS Interconnect works"},{"kind":"p","text":"AWS Interconnect is designed to connect your private networks in AWS with your private networks in remote locations or other cloud environments. You don’t need to think about physical networking devices or configure routing protocols. The service is also designed to provision capacity quickly. It follows our standards for security and maximum network resiliency."},{"kind":"p","text":"To achieve these design goals, AWS and its partners have pre-provisioned capacity in each of the supported regions and locations. This capacity spans multiple network devices distributed across at least two physical buildings. These buildings have independent power and networking."},{"kind":"p","text":"All connections between the AWS network devices and the adjacent partner devices are encrypted by default. The encryption uses industry standard IEEE 802.1AE MAC Security (MACsec). The devices are configured to transmit customer traffic only if the encryption session is active."},{"kind":"h2","text":"AWS Interconnect concepts"},{"kind":"h3","text":"Interconnect"},{"kind":"p","text":"The managed connectivity object provisioned between AWS and a provider (cloud service provider or last-mile provider). Each Interconnect is presented as a single logical object in your AWS account, abstracting the underlying redundant infrastructure."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Attach point"},{"kind":"p","text":"A logical identifier that anchors the Interconnect on each side of the connection. On AWS, the attach point is always the Direct Connect Gateway. On the remote side, the attach point varies by offering: a CSP router (Multicloud) or a partner-side network identifier (last mile)."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Direct Connect Gateway"},{"kind":"p","text":"A logical, highly-available, globally distributed object that serves as the attach point for Region or Local Zone-based AWS networking services such as Virtual Private Gateways, Transit Gateways, and Cloud WAN, as well as AWS Interconnect."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Activation Key"},{"kind":"p","text":"A token generated during the connection creation process. It is shared between parties (AWS and provider) to authorize and complete the provisioning of a new Interconnect, ensuring that both sides validate the request before resources are committed."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Create/Accept flows"},{"kind":"p","text":"The process to create a new Interconnect has two main actions: one party initiates a Create action, generating an Activation Key. The other party uses that key to perform the Accept action, triggering automated provisioning on both sides."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Built-in resiliency and security"},{"kind":"p","text":"AWS Interconnect uses redundant infrastructure to provide maximum network resiliency. The service automatically provisions multiple logical connections spanning multiple network devices across two or more physical facilities. All physical connections between AWS routers and partner devices are secured using MACsec encryption. The customer sees only a single abstracted Interconnect object in their console, while the underlying infrastructure ensures business continuity during device or facility failures."}],"lemmas":{"families":["family","families"],"managed":["managed","manage"],"manageds":["manageds","managed"],"manages":["manages","manage"],"managing":["managing","manage"],"privates":["private","privates"],"connectivities":["connectivity","connectivities"],"services":["service","services"],"serviced":["serviced","service"],"servicing":["servicing","service"],"enables":["enables","enable"],"enabled":["enabled","enable"],"enabling":["enabling","enable"],"creates":["creates","create"],"created":["created","create"],"creating":["creating","create"],"highs":["high","highs"],"higher":["higher","high"],"highest":["highest","high"],"speeds":["speed","speeds"],"speeding":["speeding","speed"],"speeded":["speeded","speed"],"connections":["connection","connections"],"locations":["location","locations"],"clouding":["clouding","cloud"],"clouded":["clouded","cloud"],"environments":["environment","environments"],"nos":["nos","no"],"longer":["longer","long"],"longers":["longer","longers"],"longs":["longs","long"],"longed":["longed","long"],"longing":["longing","long"],"needs":["need","needs"],"needed":["needed","need"],"needing":["needing","need"],"configures":["configures","configure"],"physicals":["physicals","physical"],"virtuals":["virtuals","virtual"],"routers":["router","routers"],"orders":["order","orders"],"ordered":["ordered","order"],"ordering":["ordering","order"],"crosses":["crosse","cross","crosses"],"crossing":["crossing","cross"],"crossed":["crossed","cross"],"connects":["connects","connect"],"connecting":["connecting","connect"],"connected":["connected","connect"],"peering":["peering","peer"],"peerings":["peerings","peering"],"peers":["peer","peers"],"peered":["peered","peer"],"simplified":["simplified","simplify"],"simplifieds":["simplifieds","si